WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.370
An AI-generated patch passed 155
selected upstream tests.

00:00:05.963 --> 00:00:09.690
But the repair was incomplete.
Independent behavioral checks

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.130
caught what those tests missed.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:16.090
The task comes from Boltons, a Python
utility library.

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:20.750
Its ordered multi-dictionary must accept
one-shot inputs without

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.270
inconsistent state. Empty input must add
nothing.

00:00:25.883 --> 00:00:29.883
We begin with a genuine retained model
output, backed by its

00:00:29.893 --> 00:00:31.163
captured generation evidence.

00:00:31.859 --> 00:00:35.623
It changes the main dictionary
implementation. The requirement

00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:39.963
also covers a standalone copy in the URL
utility module.

00:00:41.717 --> 00:00:45.157
Here, I import the exact retained patch
bytes through the

00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:46.337
supported local workflow.

00:00:46.882 --> 00:00:50.597
Importing checks the patch against the
approved task source. It

00:00:50.607 --> 00:00:51.837
does not execute the candidate.

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:57.130
The imported copy is labeled user-
declared and unverified.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:02.330
Matching code does not recreate captured
authorship evidence. Its

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:06.370
software candidate identity stays the
same; its provenance record

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:06.950
is separate.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:12.430
The patch materializes the input once,
returns early when it is

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.630
empty, then reuses those values.

00:01:15.266 --> 00:01:19.190
That is a sensible correction, but this
change touches only the

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:19.910
main implementation.

00:01:21.817 --> 00:01:25.597
Before execution, the owner reviews this
exact patch and

00:01:25.607 --> 00:01:27.677
explicitly authorizes the pinned task.

00:01:28.241 --> 00:01:32.877
The application supports four approved
historical Python tasks. It

00:01:32.887 --> 00:01:36.277
does not accept arbitrary repositories
or shell commands.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:42.470
Now the real audit starts. Ordinary
tests run first, then

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.030
independent behavioral checks, in
separate restricted containers.

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:50.550
The interface shows one running state
until completed evidence is

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:50.960
published.

00:01:52.583 --> 00:01:56.363
The ordinary log confirms 155 tests
passed.

00:01:56.981 --> 00:02:00.683
That is the selected upstream suite for
this task, not a claim

00:02:00.693 --> 00:02:02.363
that every possible test passed.

00:02:04.083 --> 00:02:07.843
Independent checks nevertheless fail.
The audit retains both

00:02:07.853 --> 00:02:11.863
outcomes rather than reducing them to a
single reassuring badge.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:16.163
The additional information is specific:
the required standalone

00:02:16.173 --> 00:02:17.703
behavior is still broken.

00:02:19.417 --> 00:02:22.897
The main dictionary file was repaired.
The standalone

00:02:22.907 --> 00:02:26.977
implementation in the URL utility file
was left unchanged.

00:02:27.957 --> 00:02:31.817
Normal package imports use the repaired
class; standalone mode

00:02:31.827 --> 00:02:33.337
still uses the missed copy.

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:38.590
Here is the clearest observation: empty
input should leave length

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:39.000
zero.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:43.310
Instead, the standalone object reports
length one, while its

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.530
returned keys and items are empty. Its
views of the same state

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:47.940
disagree.

00:02:49.683 --> 00:02:53.303
An iterator is consumed by the first
loop, so the second update

00:02:53.313 --> 00:02:53.943
sees nothing.

00:02:54.523 --> 00:02:58.443
An empty generator is truthy, creating a
key before the method

00:02:58.453 --> 00:03:00.003
discovers there are no values.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:05.770
The detailed checks exercise several
related cases. They describe

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:09.690
one incomplete repair, not five distinct
AI defects.

00:03:10.399 --> 00:03:14.490
This example is development evidence,
not a general model accuracy

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:15.250
statistic.

00:03:17.117 --> 00:03:19.697
Next, we load a different genuine
retained patch.

00:03:20.337 --> 00:03:24.077
Its captured generation procedure
differs, so these two examples

00:03:24.087 --> 00:03:28.017
are not a controlled vendor comparison.
We are comparing their

00:03:28.027 --> 00:03:29.497
behavior on this named task.

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:34.250
This correction updates both the main
and standalone

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:35.010
implementations.

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:40.390
Each materializes the input once,
rejects an empty addition, and

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:43.110
reuses the same values for both internal
updates.

00:03:44.883 --> 00:03:49.203
After review and explicit confirmation,
we run a fresh local audit

00:03:49.213 --> 00:03:50.563
of that retained candidate.

00:03:51.265 --> 00:03:55.023
This is another execution of existing
code, not a new model

00:03:55.033 --> 00:03:55.983
generation trial.

00:03:57.617 --> 00:04:01.617
The complete patch passes the selected
ordinary suite and all

00:04:01.627 --> 00:04:03.657
fifteen independent observation groups.

00:04:04.211 --> 00:04:08.357
That supports this task-specific
conclusion. It does not establish

00:04:08.367 --> 00:04:10.277
universal correctness or security.

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:15.490
Behind each result are the exact
candidate and source identities,

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:20.170
verifier identity, runtime image, and
execution details.

00:04:20.842 --> 00:04:24.410
The separate containers restrict
execution; they are not a

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.410
complete anti-tampering boundary for
malicious code.

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:32.110
Download the exact patch and the
complete audit evidence,

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:35.630
including logs and expected-versus-
observed behavior.

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:40.270
The reproduction instructions use the
supported local stack and

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:42.750
require a new explicit execution
confirmation.

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:49.230
The audit also persists in history.
Reopen its saved link and the

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:50.910
same evidence is available again.

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:55.610
A new execution gets a new audit
identifier; it does not become

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:57.730
another independent defect discovery.

00:04:59.483 --> 00:05:01.643
Now we switch to the actual public
showcase.

00:05:02.543 --> 00:05:06.423
Anyone can inspect the two selected
historical audits without an

00:05:06.433 --> 00:05:11.003
AI API key, Docker access, or privileged
code execution.

00:05:12.717 --> 00:05:16.417
The recorded banner matters. These are
saved historical results,

00:05:16.777 --> 00:05:18.997
not fresh audits or animated execution.

00:05:19.777 --> 00:05:24.397
Both examples expose their exact patches
and evidence. New patch

00:05:24.407 --> 00:05:27.657
import and execution remain in the local
application.

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:32.590
AI can write the patch. Patch Audit
checks the evidence.

00:05:33.186 --> 00:05:37.610
Explore the public recorded showcase, or
open the repository to

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:41.690
reproduce the local workflow. The exact
code and the limits are

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:42.510
there to inspect.
